WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.922 --> 00:00:06.331
[CINEMATIC MUSIC]

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.480
During the time of the dinosaurs,

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.320
the skies were dominated by flying reptiles

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.900
called pterosaurs.

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:33.790
There were over 250 species, and some of them were enormous.

00:00:37.468 --> 00:00:39.260
One of the most interesting because they're

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.320
the most bizarre are the azhdarchid pterosaurs.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.890
And to try and describe what an azhdarchid pterosaur was

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.540
like is very difficult. They stand as tall as giraffes.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.580
They've got heads that may have been 2 meters long,

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.607
wingspans of 10 meters.

00:00:56.607 --> 00:00:58.440
They're just some of the most bizarre things

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.690
to have ever evolved on Earth.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:08.930
Despite their size, these giants were well adapted for flight.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.435
So why do we think they hunted on the ground?

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:18.620
The azhdarchid's wing is a skin membrane supported entirely

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:23.180
by a super enlarged fourth finger.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.550
This skin membrane extended from the tip

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:27.920
of that giant fourth finger all the way

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.880
to the top of the hind leg.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.400
Azhdarchids were supremely adapted for covering

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.380
great distance, but they weren't the kind

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.990
of animals that were twisting and turning and making

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:40.820
agile movements actually in flight.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.100
This would have made it hard for azhdarchids

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.390
to catch other flying animals.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:54.400
They must have had a different way to hunt.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.970
One of the most prevalent ideas about how azhdarchid pterosaurs

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:01.930
may have fed is by using this bizarre mechanism of foraging

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.360
known as skim feeding.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:10.139
Today birds like black skimmers feed in this way.

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:11.790
It's the most ridiculous way of getting

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.027
your food you can imagine.

00:02:13.027 --> 00:02:15.360
It is literally pushing your lower jaw through the water

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.870
and crashing into your food.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.470
A skimmers neck is built to absorb sudden impacts.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:30.890
But fossils revealed azhdarchids had none of these adaptations.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.260
In fact, they would almost certainly

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:34.880
break their necks from just the strain

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.088
of trying to push their lower jaws through the water.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.810
An azhdarchid had no option.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.825
It must have landed to find food.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.480
But could it really walk fast enough to hunt?

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.290
Scientists needed evidence to persuade them

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:03.500
that it could do anything more than just waddle.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:09.810
That evidence comes from 66 million year old footprints.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.820
The biggest pterosaur track in the world

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.745
was made by a walking giant azhdarchid.

00:03:14.745 --> 00:03:16.710
And that's not only a cool thing to have,

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.810
it tells us an awful lot about how efficient

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.280
they were at walking.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:25.880
These tracks show they held their limbs directly

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:32.530
under their body giving them an upright stance.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:36.010
Their feet were padded.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.610
And unlike the small pterosaurs, their long legs

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.650
gave them an enormous stride.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.820
Although azhdarchids are peculiar looking animals,

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.290
they're moving very efficiently.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:56.580
Azhdarchids combined this very competent terrestrial walking

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.450
ability with this massive long stork-like face.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.850
They almost certainly were predators

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.420
that walked around reaching down to grab animals.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.840
We have to remember this throat size of this thing

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.220
is half a meter wide.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:18.339
That's about the width of my shoulders.

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:20.917
We would be on the menu for these things

00:04:20.917 --> 00:04:21.959
if they were alive today.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:34.970
Pterosaurs would have been an awe-inspiring sight in the sky,

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:39.050
but these giants were even more impressive and terrifying

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.310
on the ground.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.360
[CINEMATIC MUSIC]

